WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.198 --> 00:00:06.006
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:10.530
Imagine, There's a mountain in front
of you that you need to climb.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:15.260
The ground is covered with stones and
sharp rocks.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:16.688
Above you can see snow.

00:00:16.688 --> 00:00:20.890
But, you're barefoot.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:23.825
And the only shoes you have
are a pair of stiletoes.

00:00:23.825 --> 00:00:26.515
That are three sizes too small.

00:00:26.515 --> 00:00:29.190
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What would you do?

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:31.760
Would you crawl?

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.600
Ask someone to carry you?

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:39.447
Just give up?

00:00:39.447 --> 00:00:43.825
That's what's like to use
a wheel chair that hasn't been

00:00:43.825 --> 00:00:48.933
customized to your body,
your environment, and your lifestyle.

00:00:48.933 --> 00:00:52.876
And that's what life is like for
52 million people every day, but

00:00:52.876 --> 00:00:54.270
it doesn't need to be.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:01:01.860
We're going to look at how we can disrupt
assumptions, disrupt manufacturer,

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.610
and disrupt perceptions of wheel
chairs and the people who use them.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.490
So let's disrupt assumptions.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:14.080
I want to start with
the quick share of hands.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.251
How many of you have
ever been backpacking?

00:01:17.849 --> 00:01:19.000
Okay this is very impressive.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.060
It's pretty much the entire room.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.539
And how many of you have ever bought
a lonely planet travel guide?

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:28.570
That's actually way more
impressive [LAUGH],

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:30.640
that is pretty much everyone, too.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.950
Okay, so
that happens to be my go to guide.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.170
And the first one I ever bought,
it was for

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:40.632
a sabbatical from work that I planned to
spend backpacking around South East Asia.

00:01:40.632 --> 00:01:44.715
I started with a guide to Thailand,
and there were temples.

00:01:44.715 --> 00:01:46.720
There were elephant sanctuaries.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.340
I couldn't have been more excited,
so I planned my itinerary and

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:52.180
I booked my tickets.

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:57.900
Well, it was only then that I stumbled
across a chapter hidden at the back,

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:00.420
the accessibility section.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:07.270
But the advice was unequivocal, Thailand
was not a suitable destination for

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:08.060
a disabled traveler.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.060
I might find wheelchair access in some
of the biggest hotels in larger cities,

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.030
perhaps in some of the major resorts.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:18.041
But other than that, it was no go.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.780
But when I started to think about it,
it just didn't make any sense.

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:28.685
Because according to
the World Health Organization,

00:02:28.685 --> 00:02:31.810
1% of the population needs
to use a wheelchair.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.000
So if my calculations in
the countries I've found to visit,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.920
there should be one wheelchair user for
every 99 people I saw walking.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:45.580
And if they could find a way to get
around, then I could too, surely.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:49.458
So I ignore the advice and
I got on the plane.

00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:57.270
It was awesome and
accessible in its own way.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:02.140
I strapped my wheelchair to the top
of buses, to the back of motorbikes.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:04.360
I climbed mountains on donkeys,
I crossed rivers on kayaks.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:11.180
And surprisingly, one of the most
remote places I ever visited

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.700
turned out to be one of
the most accessible.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:19.500
A small rural village in northern Laos
had dirt tracks instead of roads.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.450
So there weren't any sidewalks for
me to have to navigate.

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:26.386
All the houses were just
built on a single floor.

00:03:26.386 --> 00:03:28.310
So there weren't any steps for
me to have to climb.

00:03:30.038 --> 00:03:33.758
And the more I traveled,
the more it started to occur to me.

00:03:33.758 --> 00:03:38.415
But often, the places with the least
infrastructure were actually

00:03:38.415 --> 00:03:40.710
the easiest for me to get around.

00:03:42.885 --> 00:03:44.360
But that was something really strange.

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:50.850
In two months of traveling,
I only saw two other wheelchair users,

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.720
and neither of them were
wheeling themselves.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:58.290
They were both being pushed in big,
heavy, clunky, hospital wheelchairs.

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.524
But you could never wheel yourself in.

00:04:01.524 --> 00:04:03.309
So I started to investigate.

00:04:04.995 --> 00:04:10.232
The people and organisations that I met
explained that to give independence,

00:04:10.232 --> 00:04:14.833
a wheel chair needs to be customized
to the body, the lifestyle,and

00:04:14.833 --> 00:04:20.410
the environment of its user with
traditional manufacturing techniques.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:21.690
The cost of this is prohibitive.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:26.980
A fully customized wheel chair like
mine costs about $3,000, in the country

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:33.160
like Laos that is six times
the annual average salary.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:34.220
It's just not affordable.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:40.888
And according to the World Health
Organization, worldwide, there are 52

00:04:40.888 --> 00:04:46.036
million people who need a wheelchair but
can't afford what they meets their needs.

00:04:48.428 --> 00:04:52.241
It wasn't just about
the disrupting assumptions.

00:04:52.241 --> 00:04:53.984
We needed to disrupt manufacture.

00:04:55.877 --> 00:04:57.650
I struggled with the problem for months.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:03.900
Then in December 2015, I happened
to visit an organization in Jordan.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:08.399
They were using 3D printing to support
refugees from the Syrian crisis.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:13.850
They showed me a fully customized
prosthetic hand that they made for

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:15.580
a boy who lost his
fingers in the conflict.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:19.727
My first question?

00:05:19.727 --> 00:05:22.775
How much did it cost?

00:05:22.775 --> 00:05:25.510
$39 they said.

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:27.742
Impossible.

00:05:27.742 --> 00:05:29.999
They explained that 3D printing and

00:05:29.999 --> 00:05:34.530
digital fabrication are ways to make
customized products more cheaply.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:37.410
Still, I wasn't convinced.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:41.000
But what about the design costs?

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:43.820
Well, they weren't any.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:47.559
They used an open source design,
freely available for

00:05:47.559 --> 00:05:51.207
anyone anywhere to access it out and use.

00:05:51.207 --> 00:05:52.233
Had to do?

00:05:52.233 --> 00:05:57.578
And the boy's arm measurements and
press print.

00:05:57.578 --> 00:06:01.918
Seeing the potential, I also would
anything similar with wheel chairs, and

00:06:01.918 --> 00:06:03.950
their answer is simple.

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:04.450
No.

00:06:06.145 --> 00:06:07.960
I was blown away.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:10.738
So I flew back to Europe and
I started to organize hackathons,

00:06:10.738 --> 00:06:15.690
bringing together wheelchair users,
designers, makers, engineers,

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:17.810
artists, students,
anyone who was interested.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:24.830
During our first hackathon, we scanned one
of the participants back and butt sent

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:29.000
the information through to a email, just
like you'd send a document to a printer.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:34.880
And we milled the exact contours of his
body out of a piece of high density foam

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:37.720
to create a highly personalized seat and
back rest.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:42.450
We also designed a lightweight
wheelchair frame,

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:45.090
using off the shelf
pieces of carbon fiber.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:50.160
That we cut to the exact leg length
of the user and join together with

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:57.190
logs that we 3D-printed to match the exact
angle of their preferred sitting position.

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:02.432
Total cost, $300.

00:07:02.432 --> 00:07:04.970
Well, we had more hackathons.

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:07.760
We put our designs online, and
our communities started to grow.

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:12.660
Currently, students from 84
universities across the world

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:14.708
are competing to design a modular system.

00:07:14.708 --> 00:07:18.987
So wheelchair parts can be
designed by different people,

00:07:18.987 --> 00:07:22.845
made in different places,
but still fit together.

00:07:26.056 --> 00:07:29.503
But the start of focus was
just on affordability.

00:07:29.503 --> 00:07:32.271
But we learned something
else from the hackathons.

00:07:32.271 --> 00:07:36.518
Even when people can afford $3,000 for
a fully customized wheelchair,

00:07:36.518 --> 00:07:38.070
they still weren't happy.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:46.316
Because $3,000 buys the ability to
customize for function, but not for form.

00:07:46.316 --> 00:07:49.136
It wasn't just about
disrupting manufacture,

00:07:49.136 --> 00:07:51.552
we needed to wait to disrupt perception.

00:07:55.217 --> 00:07:59.340
So, I was eight years old,
lying on the black of the couch.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:05.710
I was doing a loss line to tiny laces,
Mr Robinson got up off his chair and

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:10.640
he looked at me saying, Rachael,
you need to wear glasses.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:14.620
When I jumped off the chair,
I ran into the store and

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:16.890
I started trying on frame
after frame after frame.

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:19.170
I was elated and

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:24.020
I have never felt more glamorous than
I did back then aged eight years old,

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:29.665
admiring my reflection with my brand new,
hot pink plastic glasses.

00:08:29.665 --> 00:08:35.990
[LAUGH] Ten years later,
I was often lying down.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:38.490
This time it was a hospital bed.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:41.020
This time is different.

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:44.870
Again, a doctor got and he leaned over me.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:51.115
This time he paused before
saying Rachel you're going to

00:08:51.115 --> 00:08:55.679
need to use this wheel chair,
but this time there was no

00:08:55.679 --> 00:09:00.544
choice frames coz the therapist
ordered my wheel chair and

00:09:00.544 --> 00:09:06.124
the first time I saw myself sitting
in it I didn't recognize myself

00:09:09.825 --> 00:09:13.145
But all glasses and
wheelchairs really so different?

00:09:15.184 --> 00:09:18.457
It's not just perceptions of how I look.

00:09:18.457 --> 00:09:22.049
It's also perceptions of who I am.

00:09:22.049 --> 00:09:24.528
I'm going to let you into a little secret.

00:09:24.528 --> 00:09:29.227
People often tell me I am inspirational
because I use a wheelchair, yet

00:09:29.227 --> 00:09:31.195
I backpack around the world.

00:09:31.195 --> 00:09:33.747
I use a wheelchair, yet I have a job.

00:09:33.747 --> 00:09:37.514
I use a wheelchair, yet I drive a car.

00:09:37.514 --> 00:09:42.705
But back when I was just a glasses wearer,
no one ever told me I inspired them.

00:09:42.705 --> 00:09:48.019
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But why can't we see

00:09:48.019 --> 00:09:53.356
people who have their wheels
like people who have glasses.

00:09:53.356 --> 00:09:56.763
We've looked at how we
can disrupt assumptions,

00:09:56.763 --> 00:09:59.277
how we can disrupt manufacture, and

00:09:59.277 --> 00:10:04.318
how we can disrupt receptions of wheel
chairs and the people who use them.

00:10:04.318 --> 00:10:05.670
Let's disrupt disability.

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:09.870
Imagine.
There's a mountain in front of you that

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:11.440
you need to climb.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:14.420
The ground is covered with stones and
sharp rocks.

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:15.666
Above, you can see snow.

00:10:15.666 --> 00:10:20.899
But, this time you put
on your hiking boots or

00:10:20.899 --> 00:10:25.462
maybe your wheels, and what do you do?

00:10:25.462 --> 00:10:27.429
You climb.

00:10:27.429 --> 00:10:35.751
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

